  "summary": "A modernist movement in American painting that emerged after World War I, characterized by precise geometric forms and industrial subjects.",
  "description": "Characterized by clean lines, simplified geometric forms, and a focus on industrial and architectural subjects, Precisionism celebrated American industry and technology while maintaining a cool, detached perspective. It emerged as a distinctly American response to European Cubism and Futurism, often depicting factories, skyscrapers, and machinery with precise, almost machine-like rendering. The movement influenced later American abstract art and Pop Art.",
  "culturalContext": "Precisionism (1920s-30s) — Charles Sheeler, Charles Demuth, Georgia O'Keeffe's city years, Louis Lozowick — rendered America's machines, factories and skyscrapers as crystalline planar geometry: Sheeler's Ford photographs and \"American Landscape\", Demuth's \"I Saw the Figure 5 in Gold\", grain elevators as cathedrals. The first fully American modernist movement, it worshipped industrial form without European angst, and its clean Cubist-Calisthenics still frames corporate American \"efficiency\" aesthetics.",
